{:edb/db-before-t 2070 :edb/db-after-t 2071 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535383 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:13:04.510Z" 13194139535383 true] [17592186087531 1100 "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0$. If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda$?" 13194139535383 false] [17592186087531 1100 "Consider the equation $y''-2y'+y = 0.$ If $y = e^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then what is $\\lambda?$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186087531 1145 "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} - 2λe^{λx} + e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} - 2λ + 1) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 2λ + 1 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(λ - 1)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda = 1$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation." 13194139535383 false] [17592186087531 1145 "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-2\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =  1$  is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation." 13194139535383 true] [17592186087532 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0.\n$$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186087532 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$\\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2} - 12\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t} + 36y =0$." 13194139535383 false] [17592186087532 1145 "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λt} - 12λe^{λt} + 36e^{λt} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λt}(λ^{2} - 12λ + 36) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} - 12λ + 36 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ - 6)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda = 6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}\n$$" 13194139535383 false] [17592186087532 1145 "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d} y}{\\textrm{d} t}= \\lambda e^{\\lambda t},\\qquad \\frac{\\textrm{d}^2 y}{\\textrm{d} t^2}= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+36{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =  6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{6 t} + Bte^{6t}.\n$$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186087533 1100 "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$  for $y=y(x),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n- $xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n\n- $2e^{6x}$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186087533 1100 "Given the equation $y''+12y'+36y = 0$ for $y=y(x)$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?\n\n1. $xe^{-6x}$\n2. $4e^{-6x}-3xe^{-6x}$\n3. $2e^{6x}$" 13194139535383 false] [17592186087533 1145 "Assuming\n\n$$\ny=e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nand differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2}e^{λx} + 12λe^{λx} + 36e^{λx} &= 0 \\\\\ne^{λx}(λ^{2} + 12λ + 36) &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nλ^{2} + 12λ + 36 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 6)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root. Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3$. So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x}$, which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535383 false] [17592186087533 1145 "Assuming $y=e^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny'= \\lambda e^{\\lambda x},\\qquad y''= \\lambda^2 e^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}+12\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+36{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}+12\\lambda +36 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +6)^{2} & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand $\\lambda =-6$ is a repeated root.  Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{-6 x} + Bxe^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to determine whether each of the given functions takes the form shown above.\n\n- Function I takes the above form with $A=0$ and $B=1.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function II takes the above form with $A=4$ and $B=-3.$ So, it is a solution to the given ODE.\n\n- Function III does *not* take the above form. It contains $e^{6x},$ which is not included in the above form. So, it is *not* a solution to the given ODE.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." 13194139535383 true] [17592186087713 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346799 13194139535383 true] [17592186087713 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346806 13194139535383 true] [17592186087713 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346813 13194139535383 true] [17592186087713 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346820 13194139535383 true] [17592186087713 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346869 13194139535383 true] [17592186087714 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346827 13194139535383 true] [17592186087714 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346834 13194139535383 true] [17592186087714 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346841 13194139535383 true] [17592186087714 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346876 13194139535383 true] [17592186087715 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346848 13194139535383 true] [17592186087715 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346855 13194139535383 true] [17592186087715 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346862 13194139535383 true] [17592186087715 1020 #edb/ref 17592186346883 13194139535383 true] [17592186088181 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535383 true] [17592186088181 1100 "Given the equation $y^{″} - 14y^{′} + 49y = 0$ for $y = y(t)$, which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139535383 false] [17592186088181 1100 "Given the equation ${y}^{″}-14{y}^{′}+49y=0$ for $y=y(t),$ which of the following is **not** a solution to this equation?" 13194139535383 true] [17592186088181 1145 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda t}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda t}-14\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda t}+49{e}^{\\lambda t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda t}({\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-14\\lambda +49 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -7)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =7$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation.\n\nTherefore, any linear combination of the functions ${e}^{7t}$ and $t{e}^{7t}$ is a solution to the original differential equation. \n\nAmong the given options, only $7{e}^{t}+t{e}^{7t}$ is not a solution." 13194139535383 true] [17592186088291 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535383 true] [17592186088291 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}-3\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{9}{4}y=0.\n$$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186088291 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$\\frac{d^{2}y}{dx^{2}} - 3\\frac{dy}{dx} + \\frac{9}{4}y = 0$." 13194139535383 false] [17592186088291 1145 "Let $y={e}^{\\lambda x}.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-3\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+\\frac{9}{4}{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda +\\frac{9}{4}=0.\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ {(\\lambda -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda =\\frac{3}{2}$ is a repeated root.\n\nSo the general solution to the differential equation is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x/2}+Bx{e}^{3x/2}.\n$$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186346800 13194139535383 true] [17592186346799 1089 "q-130129" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535383 true] [17592186346799 1100 "Consider the equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=0.$ If $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ is a solution to the equation, then $\\lambda =$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346799 1113 #uuid "3d136bdf-fdec-5c73-91c2-2820e0f1b6e1" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346799 1145 "Assuming $y={e}^{\\lambda x}$ and differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x},\\,{y}^{″}={\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}{e}^{\\lambda x}-4\\lambda {e}^{\\lambda x}+4{e}^{\\lambda x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{\\lambda x}({\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe auxiliary equation is therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }^{2}-4\\lambda +4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the auxiliary equation results in\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda -2)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand so $\\lambda =2$ is a repeated root of the auxiliary equation." 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346801 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346802 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346803 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346804 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186346805 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1041 "selection" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186346803 13194139535383 true] [17592186346800 1110 #uuid "cb03e576-60f5-578f-b423-8382ef24a475" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346801 1034 "$-2$ or $2$" 13194139535383 true] [17592186346801 1103 #edb